1. Requirements

a. Functional Requirements
    Below are the requirements and their description.
      i. Authentication - The user can register and login account in software.
      ii. Create a Social Media Post - Users can create / edit / delete social media post. A social media post can be Text post, Image post or a Video post. 
      iii. Like / Unlike Posts - Users can like / Unlike other people's post.
      iv. Follow / Unfollow - Users can follow / unfollow users.
      v. Comment on Posts - Users can comment on each other posts.
      vi. News Feed - Users can view news feed. The feed shows posts from people they follow, listed from newest to oldest.
      vii. Like / Unlike Graph - Users can see users like / unlike graph in dashboard.
      viii. Followers Graph - Users can see followers Increasing / Decreasing graph in dashboard.
      ix. Engagement Graph - Users can see comments / engagements Graph (comments directly proportional to engagements)

  b. Non-Functional Requirements
 
      i. Eventual Consistency - If the user posts something, it should show up within 2 seconds on News Feed.
      ii. Like / Unlike Update - If the user like / unlike on post, it should update like / unlike graph in dashboard within 2 seconds.
      iii. Follow / Unfollow Update - If the user follows a user, it should update follow / unfollow graph in dashboard within 2 seconds.
      iv. Engagement Update - If a user comments on the post, it should update users engagement graph in dashboard within 2 seconds.
      v. Usability - The rendering should be fast. Example: It's frustrating if a post loads in parts (like the image first, then text, etc.). The post should appear almost instantly.
      vi. Load Latency - The News Feed should load within 1-2 seconds

  c. Architectue Requirements
 
      i. Extensibility - The design of our system should be such that it is easier to extend it in the future. Example: If we need to add features like replying to a comment, post recommendations, or ads.
      ii. Modularity - The system should split in small independent "modules".
      iii. Cohesion and Coupling - The system should have "high cohesion" and "low coupling".
      iv. Microservice - The system should use small independent services, each one runs on its own, focuses on one job.

2. Capacity Estimation (Optional)
  Daily Active Users - 500 million
  Monthly Active Users - 2 billion

  i. Throughput
    Writes:
      * Create a Social Media Post
        Assume 10% of DAU create social media post.
        a. Total DAU = 500 million
        b. 10% of DAU create post - 50 million
        c. This results in 50 million 'create post' requests per day.
      
      * Like / Unlike
        Assume user likes 5 posts per day.
        a. Total DAU = 500 million
        b. 500 million x 5 = 2,500 million.
        c. This results in 2500 million requests per day.

      * Follow / Unfollow
        Assume 5% of DAU users perform a follow / unfollow action.
        a. Total DAU = 500 million
        b. 500 million x (5/100) = 25 million requests per day.
        c. This results in 25 million requests per day.

      * Comment on Posts
        Assume 2 comments by user daily.
        a. Total DAU = 500 million
        b. 500 x 2 = 1000 million requests per day.
        c. This results in 1 billion requests per day.

    Reads
      * News Feed
        Assume an average user reads 100 posts per day.
        b. Total DAU = 500 million
        c. Each user reads 100 posts per day
        c. 500 million × 100 = 50000 million read requests per day.

  ii. Storage Estimation
    Post Data
      Text posts are usually around 100 KB, image posts around 0.5 MB, and video posts around 20 MB. From throughput estimation, we know there are 50 million ‘create post’ requests per day.
        Now, assume:
          20% of posts are text posts
          60% of posts are image posts
          20% of posts are video posts
      
        Storage Calculation:
          Text Data Storage - (0.2 * 50M) * 100KB = 1TB/day
          Image Data Storage - (0.6 * 50M) * 0.5MB = 15TB/day
          Video Data Storage - (0.2 * 50M) * 20MB = 200TB/day
          Total Storage - 1TB + 15TB + 200TB = 216TB/day

3. API Design
  We will use the REST API for this communication. Here are the technical details:
    Register:
      HTTP Method: POST
      Endpoint: /v1/auth/register
    Login:
      HTTP Method: POST
      Endpoint: /v1/auth/login
    Text Post:
      Endpoint: /v1/posts
      HTTP Method: POST
      HTTP Body: {"userId": "Your unique identifier", "text": "The content of your post", "hashtags": ["The tags you include in your post"]}
    Image or Video Post:
      Endpoint: /v1/posts
      HTTP Method: POST
      HTTP Body: {"userId": "Your unique identifier", "mediaUrls": "The web address (URL) where your uploaded image or video is stored.", "description": "The caption you write under your image or video", "hashtags": ["The tags you include in your post."]}
    Like  or Unlike:
      Endpoint: /v1/like, /v1/unlike
      HTTP Method: POST, DELETE
      HTTP Body: {"userId: "Your unique identifier", "postId": "The unique identifier of the post you are liking on"}, {"userId": "Your unique identifier", "postId": "The unique identifier of the post you are unliking on"}
    Comments:
      Endpoint: /v1/comment
      HTTP Method: POST
      HTTP Body: {"userId": "Your unique identifier", "postId": "The unique identifier of the post you are commenting on", "comment": "The text of your comment"}
    Follow or Unfollow:
      Endpoint: /v1/follow, /v1/unfollow
      HTTP Method: POST, DELETE
      HTTP Body: {"followerId": "Your unique identifier", "followeeId": "The unique identifier of the user you want to follow"}, {"followerId": "Your unique identifier", "followeeId": "The unique identifier of the user you want to unfollow"}
    News Feed:
      Endpoint: /v1/feeds/{userId}
      HTTP Method: GET
      HTTP Body: NONE

4. High Level Design
  1. Create a Social Media Text Post
    * When the user writes a text post and clicks submit, a POST Request is sent with the details of the text post.
    * The API Gateway handles the incoming request and routes it to the Post Writer Service.
    * The Post Writer Service saves the new post in the Posts Database (Posts DB).
    * The Client receives confirmation of the successful post.

  2. Follow / Unfollow
    * When the user follows another user, a POST request is sent to the /v1/follow endpoint.
    * API Gateway Handling The API Gateway handles the incoming request and routes it to the Follow Service.
    * The Follow Service updates the Follow Database (Follow DB) to reflect the new follower / followee relationship in dashboard graph.
    * The client receives confirmation of the successful follow request.

  3. Like / Unlike
    * When the user likes another user, a POST request is sent to the /v1/like and so on endpoint.
    * API Gateway Handling The API Gateway handles the incoming request and routes it to the Like / Unlike Service.
    * The Like / Unlike Service updates the Like / Unlike Database to reflect the new like-unlike relationship in dashboard graph.
    * The client receives confirmation of the successful like / unlike request.

  4. News Feed
    * When the user tries to load their news feed, a GET request is sent to the API Gateway on the /v1/feeds/{userId} endpoint.
    * The API Gateway receives the request and forwards it to the NewsFeed Reader Service.
    * The NewsFeed Reader Service fetches the pre-built news feed from the FeedDB.
    * The NewsFeed Reader Service sends the news feed back to the user.
    * The news feed only contains the URLs for the images and videos, not the actual media files. The client fetches the actual images and videos from the Object Storage using these URLs.

  5. Create Image or Video Post
    * The client requests a pre-signed URL from the API Gateway to upload the image/video.
    * API Gateway receives this request and routes it to the Pre-Signed URL Generator Service. The Pre-Signed URL Generator Service generates the pre-signed URL and returns it to the client.
    * The client uploads the image/video directly to Object Storage using the pre-signed URL.
    * Object Storage returns the URL of the uploaded image/video to the client.
    * The client uses this uploaded image/video URL in the request body & sends a POST request to the API Gateway.
    * The API Gateway handles the incoming request and routes it to the Post Writer Service.
    * The Post Writer Service saves the new post in the Posts DB.
    * The client receives confirmation of the successful post.
    * The News Feed Generator Service fetches the new post data from the Posts DB using postId.
    * The News Feed Generator Service finds all followers for this user using Follow DB.
    * The News Feed Generator Service adds the new post to all followers' feeds. This information is stored in the Feeds DB. Each entry in the Feeds DB maps UserId to their News FeedUserId → { Post1, Post2, Post3 ... }

  6. Comment on Post
    * The user writes a comment and submits it. A POST request is sent to /v1/comments with the comment data.
    * The API Gateway handles the incoming request. It routes the request to the Comment Service.
    * Thise Comment Service processes the comment. It stores the comment in the Comments Database.
    * The Comment Service then fetces the postId and post owner and update the engagement graph in dashboard of the followeeId (owner).
    * The client receives confirmation that the comment was successfully posted.

5. Database Selection
    Database: No-SQL
      We are choosing No-SQL database because:
        i. Extensibility - Easily accommodates new media types, such as audio support, as the application scales.
        ii. Scehema Flexibility - Offers a schema-less structure, allowing documents to store varied and unstructured data without rigid predefined tables.
        iii. Fast Access - Optimized for fast data reads and writes, meeting the performance requirements of the system.
        iv. Simple Queries - Eliminates the need for complex multi-table joins by keeping related data together in documents.
        v. Frequent Evolving Data Changes - Easily adapts to shifting data requirements and new attributes over time without requiring heavy database migrations.

    Database Decision Table
      i. LikesDB
        * Deciding Factor:
          a. Extremely high scale and throughput - 2.5 billion activities/day.
          b. No fixed schema - Data may evolve in future to include like 'types' aka reactions (funny, wow, sad, love, ...).
          Decision - No-SQL
      ii. FollowDB
        * Deciding Factor:
          a. In Follow DB, we are storing user connections and relationships - follower and followee data. 
          Decision - No-SQL
      iii. CommentDB
        * Deciding Factor:
          a. Extremely high scale and throughput - 1 billion activities/day.
          b. No fixed schema - Data may evolve in future to include replies to a comment aka nested comments (comment inside a comment).
      iv. PostDB
        * Deciding Factor:
            a. Social media posts can include text, images, videos, and metadata. Some posts have only text, some have only image or video. In short, it doesn't have a fixed structure.
            b. We are only reading posts by PostID in the news feed generation flow.
            c. Simple query pattern - We only need to find all the comments for a post.
        * Decision - No-SQL
      v. FeedDB
        * Deciding Factor:
          a. Stores data as UserId -> [Post1, Post2, ...]. Post data doesn't have a fixed structure, so FeedDB is also unstructured.
          b. Simple query pattern - We are only reading news feed by userID in the news feed generation flow.
        * Decision - No-SQL

